SIG pub fn adopt_find_duplicate(existing: &str, section: &str) -> Option<(String, i64, i64)>
ABI crate shape: a LIBRARY crate ([lib], src/lib.rs exporting pub fn adopt_find_duplicate) with NO dependencies — no
  main, no I/O, no unsafe. The verifier's harness binds both texts as &str, calls the function and reshapes the tuple
  into the record in DECLARED field order: (heading, startLine, endLine); None = none. Lone surrogates have no &str form
  and are outside the primary's envelope.
MECHANISM WHITESPACE is NOT char::is_whitespace, str::trim or split_whitespace: those use Unicode White_Space, which
  includes U+0085 and excludes U+FEFF — the opposite of the pin on both. Write one predicate over the pinned set and use
  it for every trim, collapse (R5), blank test (R8), fence indent (R2) and heading test (R3). LINES (R1) =
  str::split('\n') then strip_suffix('\r') once (never lines(), which also drops a final empty line).
MECHANISM lengths (R5 "exceeds 15") count UTF-16 units = s.encode_utf16().count() (or sum of char::len_utf16), never
  str::len (bytes) or chars().count(). Byte-slicing is safe only at offsets of ASCII matches ("#", "(", ")", "-->",
  "<!--", the backticks, the tildes). The markers (R6 R7) are scans: for each "<!--" in order, skip pinned WHITESPACE, test
  the keyword, then test the WORD-CHAR boundary — that is the whole guard (R6); for the begin marker (R7) also str::find the next
  "-->" and the next "<!--" after the keyword, and accept only when the "-->" exists and comes first. The one-line-fence test
  (R2) trims with the pinned predicate and measures UTF-16 units; the fence state (R2) keeps the opener's run (char, length).
MECHANISM HEADING-KEY (R4): lowercase per char with char::to_lowercase (the full mapping: U+0130 -> "i" + U+0307,
  U+212A -> "k"), then keep ASCII a-z/0-9 and treat everything else as a separator. No regex crate is needed.
